namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TutorialMapClass Interfacing with raw buffers: the Map class, or the Lodger who Keeps his Own Furniture

This page explains how to work with "raw" C/C++ arrays, those honest, unadorned tenants of the Heap and the Stack who have never been introduced into polite society.
This can be useful in a variety of contexts, particularly when "importing" vectors and matrices from other libraries into %Eigen, which are, so to speak, foreign gentlemen arriving with their luggage already packed.

\eigenAutoToc

\section TutorialMapIntroduction Introduction

Occasionally you may have a pre-defined array of numbers that you want to use within %Eigen as a vector or matrix. While one option is to make a copy of the data (a course which is expensive, and which has ruined better men than you), most commonly you probably want to reuse this memory as an %Eigen type, without disturbing a single coefficient in its bed. Fortunately, this is very easy with the Map class.

\section TutorialMapTypes Map types and declaring Map variables

A Map object has a type defined by its %Eigen equivalent, as a gentleman's station is defined by that of his nearest relation:
\code
Map<Matrix<typename Scalar, int RowsAtCompileTime, int ColsAtCompileTime> >
\endcode
Note that, in this default case, a Map requires just a single template parameter, and is the most economical of guests.  

To construct a Map variable, you need two other pieces of information: a pointer to the region of memory defining the array of coefficients, and the desired shape of the matrix or vector; the address of the house, as it were, and the number of rooms.  For example, to define a matrix of \c float with sizes determined at compile time, you might do the following:
\code
Map<MatrixXf> mf(pf,rows,columns);
\endcode
where \c pf is a \c float \c * pointing to the array of memory.  A fixed-size read-only vector of integers might be declared as
\code
Map<const Vector4i> mi(pi);
\endcode
where \c pi is an \c int \c *. In this case the size does not have to be passed to the constructor, because it is already specified by the Matrix/Array type, and it would be the height of impertinence to ask a second time.

Note that Map does not have a default constructor; you \em must pass a pointer to initialize the object, for a Map without an address is a lodger without a lodging. However, you can work around this requirement (see \ref TutorialMapPlacementNew).

Map is flexible enough to accommodate a variety of different data representations, being a landlord of uncommon indulgence.  There are two other (optional) template parameters:
\code
Map<typename MatrixType,
    int MapOptions,
    typename StrideType>
\endcode
\li \c MapOptions specifies whether the pointer is \c #Aligned, or \c #Unaligned.  The default is \c #Unaligned, the Map assuming nothing in the way of good breeding on the part of its pointer.
\li \c StrideType allows you to specify a custom layout for the memory array, using the Stride class.  One example is
to map every other row from a column-major array with an inner stride of 2, which is to say, to visit every second house in the street:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>\include Tutorial_Map_inner_stride.cpp </td>
<td>\verbinclude Tutorial_Map_inner_stride.out </td>
</table>
However, Stride is even more flexible than this, and capable of feats of accommodation that would astonish a London hotel-keeper; for details, see the documentation for the Map and Stride classes.

\section TutorialMapUsing Using Map variables, or the Lodger at Table

You can use a Map object just like any other %Eigen type; it will sit at the family table and be helped to the same dishes:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>\include Tutorial_Map_using.cpp </td>
<td>\verbinclude Tutorial_Map_using.out </td>
</table>

All %Eigen functions are written to accept Map objects just like other %Eigen types. However, when writing your own functions taking %Eigen types, this does \em not happen automatically: a Map type is not identical to its Dense equivalent, however strong the family resemblance, and a function that demands the one will turn the other from its door.  See \ref TopicFunctionTakingEigenTypes for details.

\section TutorialMapPlacementNew Changing the mapped array: the Lodger Removes to Other Apartments

It is possible to change the array of a Map object after declaration, using the C++ "placement new" syntax; the lodger, so to speak, giving a week's notice and taking up residence at a new address:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>\include Map_placement_new.cpp </td>
<td>\verbinclude Map_placement_new.out </td>
</table>
Despite appearances, this does not invoke the memory allocator, because the syntax specifies the location for storing the result; no new house is built, and the Heap is not so much as consulted.

This syntax makes it possible to declare a Map object without first knowing the mapped array's location in memory, a lodger being engaged, in short, before the lodgings are found:
\code
Map<Matrix3f> A(NULL);  // don't try to use this matrix yet!
VectorXf b(n_matrices);
for (int i = 0; i < n_matrices; i++)
{
  new (&A) Map<Matrix3f>(get_matrix_pointer(i));
  b(i) = A.trace();
}
\endcode

*/

}
